WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.998
Which snack should the tuck shop
sell? Ask every child, and count.

2
00:00:09.218 --> 00:00:13.722
Some problems are solved by collecting
data and reading what it says.

3
00:00:14.222 --> 00:00:18.170
Three school problems. Which
snack should the tuck shop sell?

4
00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:22.105
How many chairs for the concert?
Count the parents who are coming.

5
00:00:22.325 --> 00:00:26.737
When is the playground busiest?
Count the children at each break.

6
00:00:26.957 --> 00:00:33.087
Collecting data means asking or counting.
Interpreting it means reading what the numbers say.

7
00:00:33.307 --> 00:00:38.647
Not every problem is a data problem.
What your story is about is not a count.

8
00:00:39.147 --> 00:00:44.302
Eight children answered two questions
on a tablet. How many pets do you have?

9
00:00:44.522 --> 00:00:49.816
Discrete data is whole numbers you
count. Zero pets, one pet, two pets.

10
00:00:50.036 --> 00:00:53.473
Never one and a half. You
cannot have half a cat.

11
00:00:53.693 --> 00:01:00.241
The second question: which pet would
you most like? Cat, dog, fish or rabbit.

12
00:01:00.461 --> 00:01:04.919
Those are categories, not
counts. That is categorical data.

13
00:01:05.139 --> 00:01:08.715
The form on the tablet counted
each answer as it came in.

14
00:01:09.215 --> 00:01:13.487
Some data is a name. Red,
blue, dog, cat, came by bus.

15
00:01:13.707 --> 00:01:18.444
You cannot add a cat to a dog. You
can only count how many of each.

16
00:01:18.664 --> 00:01:23.169
Other data is numerical. It is a
number you counted or measured.

17
00:01:23.389 --> 00:01:28.822
How many pets, how tall, how many minutes.
Numbers can be added and put in order.

18
00:01:29.322 --> 00:01:33.595
Here is the pet data, and a digital
tool can show it three ways.

19
00:01:33.815 --> 00:01:39.341
A table gives the exact numbers. Two
children have no pets, three have one.

20
00:01:39.561 --> 00:01:44.809
A bar chart shows the biggest at a
glance. One pet has the tallest bar.

21
00:01:45.029 --> 00:01:50.230
A pictogram draws one picture for
each answer. You count the pictures.

22
00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:55.559
Categories work too. Four children
chose a cat, and two chose a dog.

23
00:01:55.779 --> 00:01:59.865
The three views never disagree.
They are the same eight answers.

24
00:02:00.365 --> 00:02:03.059
Each view is best for
a different question.

25
00:02:03.279 --> 00:02:07.040
Exactly how many children have
two pets? The table says two.

26
00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:12.833
Which pet is the class favourite? The
bar chart shows it at a glance: cat.

27
00:02:13.053 --> 00:02:18.672
Count them by eye? The pictogram.
Interpreting is reading the right view.

28
00:02:19.172 --> 00:02:24.699
Collect the data, then read what it
says. That is a problem solved with data.

29
00:02:24.919 --> 00:02:29.145
Counts are discrete data.
Categories are categorical data.

30
00:02:29.365 --> 00:02:34.380
A table gives the exact numbers. A bar
chart shows the biggest at a glance.

31
00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:39.244
A pictogram gives one picture for each
one, so you can count them by eye.
